**Current support versus accepted design.** The Alpha compiler accepts an optional `config.moth` with parenthesised grouped `project` and `html` records and declaration-owned `#Config of T` inputs. A missing `config.moth` uses validated builder defaults, a directory-derived project name and no version. Closed `$project` / `$html_builder` configuration and `$config` prefixes remain accepted queued design. `entry_root` is retired from both the operational and future contracts. The module diagnostic limit below now governs file preparation and ordinary-body diagnostic recovery. The project diagnostic threshold is a soft scheduling cutoff for new work, and skipped work is reported as not checked. `moth new` does not yet emit the starter below. See @../progress (Progress) and @../directives (Directives).

`config.moth` is optional. When present, it lives at the selected project root, is loaded before source-tree discovery and is one self-contained compile-time source file with no source dependencies, package resolution or authored file-value paths. A nested `config.moth` is inert to an enclosing build.

### Accepted project config

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    $project(
        name = "my_site",
    )

    $html_builder
]

Version is optional. To supply one, use an earlier `$config` contract or a fixed literal:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    $config
    version #String? = none

    $project(
        name = "my_site",
        version = version,
    )

    $html_builder
]

A project field named `version` does not satisfy a source input contract. Do not treat `"0.1.0"` as a `$project` default.

### The `$project` directive

`config.moth` contains exactly one `$project` invocation. The positional order is:

[table:
    [th:
        [: Parameter]
        [: Type]
        [: Default]
        [: Configurable value permitted]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `name`]
        [: `String`]
        [: The selected directory name]
        [: No]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `version`]
        [: `String?`]
        [: `none`]
        [: Yes]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `author`]
        [: `String?`]
        [: `none`]
        [: Yes]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `license`]
        [: `String?`]
        [: `none`]
        [: Yes]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `template_const_loop_iteration_limit`]
        [: `I32`]
        [: The existing compiler-owned default]
        [: No]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `max_errors_per_module`]
        [: `I32?`]
        [: `5`]
        [: No]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `max_errors_per_project`]
        [: `I32?`]
        [: `30`]
        [: No]
    ]
]

The signature is closed. Arbitrary fields, misspelled standard fields and `metadata = ...` are rejected. `@project` publishes the predefined fields except the two diagnostic limits. The diagnostic limits are compiler controls and are never published.

`name`, `template_const_loop_iteration_limit`, `max_errors_per_module` and `max_errors_per_project` remain fixed-only, including through helper constants. An omitted `name` defaults to the selected directory name and is validated as a Moth project identifier; it is never silently rewritten. Omitted `version` means unversioned.

### Diagnostic limits

`max_errors_per_module` is a strict allowance of primary errors retained for each module. File preparation keeps the first errors in source order up to the allowance. Ordinary-body diagnostic recovery admits a further eligible body after the first failed one only while the retained primary error count stays below the allowance. Check-only files attributed to one module share that module's allowance.

`max_errors_per_project` is a soft scheduling threshold for the whole command. Once the returned primary errors reach it, the compiler starts no further modules, source packages or check-only files. Work already running finishes and keeps its errors, so the project total may exceed the threshold. It is not a maximum result count. Skipped work is reported as not checked and never counts as successful.

A present value must be an `I32` from 1 to 2147483647. Zero, negative values and other types are rejected. Omitting a setting uses its default. An explicit `none` disables that one count and leaves the other unchanged. Warnings, notes and related labels never count. Neither setting disables the compiler's fixed recovery-work guard or changes program meaning.

### `$html_builder`

An authored HTML directory project contains exactly one `$html_builder` invocation. Bare `$html_builder` uses defaults. `$html_builder()` is invalid. The command selects the builder first; this directive never selects a backend or platform.

The positional order and defaults are:

[table:
    [th:
        [: Parameter]
        [: Type]
        [: Default]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `dev_output`]
        [: `String`]
        [: `"_dev"`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `release_output`]
        [: `String`]
        [: `"_release"`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `origin`]
        [: `String`]
        [: `"/"`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `page_url_style`]
        [: `String`]
        [: `"trailing_slash"`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `redirect_index_html`]
        [: `Bool`]
        [: `true`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `html_lang`]
        [: `String`]
        [: `"en"`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `html_title_prefix`]
        [: `String`]
        [: `""`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `html_title_postfix`]
        [: `String`]
        [: `""`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `html_favicon`]
        [: `String?`]
        [: `none`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `html_inject_charset`]
        [: `Bool`]
        [: `true`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `html_inject_viewport`]
        [: `Bool`]
        [: `true`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `html_inject_color_scheme`]
        [: `Bool`]
        [: `true`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `html_inject_core_css`]
        [: `Bool`]
        [: `true`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `html_body_style`]
        [: `String`]
        [: `""`]
    ]
]

`page_url_style` accepts `trailing_slash`, `no_trailing_slash` or `ignore`. `html_favicon` accepts a compile-time String containing an untracked URL, not necessarily a string literal. Tracked file resources belong in a direct root builder slot because `config.moth` rejects authored file-value paths.

Unknown or inactive unsupported directives error. Multi-builder configuration waits for `$feature`. Synthetic single-file operations without authored `config.moth` keep their explicit synthetic path and do not invent `$project` / `$html_builder` source.

### Current grouped config

Operational config uses declaration-owned `#Config` inputs and parenthesised grouped records:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    version #Config of String = "0.1.0"
    project_error_limit #I32? = none

    project #= (
        name = "moth_docs",
        version = version,
        max_errors_per_module = 5,
        max_errors_per_project = project_error_limit,
    )

    html #= ()
]

The `project` record may omit `name`, `version`, or both. An omitted name uses the selected directory name; an omitted version means unversioned. The record has no `entry_root`; the selected directory is the source root. An authored HTML directory config still includes `html #= ()`; omitting the active builder section is diagnosed. Its schema defaults include `_dev` and `_release` outputs.

The grouped `project` record accepts `max_errors_per_module` and `max_errors_per_project` as fixed-only `I32?` compiler controls. Omission uses the default of 5 module errors or 30 project errors. An explicit `none` disables that one limit and leaves the other unchanged. A present value must be an exact `I32` from 1 to 2147483647. The grouped record has no field type context, so write an explicit absence through a typed `I32?` constant as the example does. Writing `none` directly in the record is a language type error. The loader validates and stores both settings today. The module limit governs file preparation and ordinary-body diagnostic recovery. The project threshold is a soft scheduling cutoff for new work in check, build and dev. When discovery ends early, one line after the diagnostics names the reason and says that later code was not checked. The CLI, the dev server's terminal and browser error page and the builder SDK's error text all show it.

Retired flat keys and `entry_root` remain rejected. Keep live `docs/config.moth` on this current path.

### Concepts related to project config

- For `$config` input contracts see @#build-inputs (Build configuration values).
- For builder slots see @#entry-config (Entry metadata and builder slots).
- For general directive grammar see @../directives (Directives).
